* {{IPA|en|/həd/|/əd/|a=weak form}}
* {{sense|had to}} {{enPR|hăt}}, {{IPA|en|/hæt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|æd|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|have||ed-form}}
#: {{ux|en|This morning I '''had''' an egg for breakfast.}}
#: {{ux|en|A good time was '''had''' by all.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Had''' you enough time to do it last night?}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Mansfield Park|chapter=I|volume=I|page=1|passage=About thirty years ago, Miss Maria Ward, of Huntingdon, with only seven thousand pounds, '''had''' the good luck to captivate Sir Thomas Bertram, of Mansfield Park, in the county of Northampton,{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary|followed by a past participle}} {{non-gloss|Used to form the [[past perfect]] tense, expressing an action that took place prior to a reference point that is itself in the past.}}
#: {{ux|en|I felt sure that I '''had''' seen him before.}}
#: {{ux|en|When I''''d''' (already) done some exercise, I had a cappuccino.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 15 2011|author=Ben Cooper|journal=w:The Guardian|location=London
|passage=Cooper seems an odd choice, but imagine if they '''had''' taken MTV's advice and chosen {{w|Robert Pattinson}}?}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary|now|_|rare}} As past [[subjunctive]]: would have.
#: {{ux|en|'''Had''' I not known you were coming [[this evening]], I'd have left at midday.}} 
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1499|author=w:John Skelton|title=The Bowge of Courte
|passage=To holde myne honde, by God, I had grete payne; / For forthwyth there I '''had''' him slayne, / But that I drede mordre wolde come oute{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|II|4|passage=''Julius Cæsar'' '''had''' escaped death, if going to the Senate-house, that day wherein he was murthered by the Conspirators, he had read a memorial which was presented unto him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1849|author=w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|title=In Memoriam|section=24
|passage=If all was good and fair we met, / This earth '''had''' been the Paradise / It never look’d to human eyes / Since our first Sun arose and set.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=w:George Bernard Shaw|title=Caesar and Cleopatra
|passage=CAESAR (smiling). Of course I '''had''' rather you stayed.}}

====Usage notes====
''Had'', like {{m|en|that}}, is one of a small number of words to be correctly used twice in succession in English in a non-contrived way, e.g. “He '''had had''' several operations previously.” Longer sequences are also grammatically possible, though generally restricted to deliberately constructed examples, such as “The lessons I '''had had had had''' no effect.”

